{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-71573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & =9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/9}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/9}+{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-71573" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71573" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/9}+{y}^{2}"] ["math" "z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}"] ["math" "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/9}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71573" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/9}+{y}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+z-2,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+z-2 & =-2 \\\\ z & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=-2$ is the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-71572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}"] ["math" "z={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4"] ["math" "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}"] ["math" "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+4"] ["math" "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x-y},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{z}{x-y} & =-1 \\\\ z & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=-1$ is the plane $z=y-x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-129183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=\\frac{1}{x+y}"] ["math" "z=\\frac{1}{x-y}"] ["math" "z=x+y"] ["math" "z=x-y"] ["math" "z=y-x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=y-x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z-1},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z-1} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z-1 & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-129186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1"] ["math" "z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2"] ["math" "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}"] ["math" "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1"] ["math" "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-129182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1"] ["math" "z=4x+y-1"] ["math" "z=4x-y-1"] ["math" "z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}"] ["math" "z=4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y+z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y+z) & =c \\\\ x-2y+z & ={e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the exponent ${e}^{c}$ is positive for all $-\\infty <c<\\infty .$ If we let ${e}^{c}={r}^{2},$ we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\nx-2y+z={r}^{2},\\,r\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-129198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A family of planes: $x-2y+z=-{r}^{2}$"] ["text" "A family of planes: $x-2y+z=0$"] ["text" "A family of planes: $x-2y+z={r}^{2}$"] ["text" "A family of surfaces: $z={e}^{-x+2y+{r}^{2}}$"] ["text" "A family of surfaces: $z={e}^{x-2y+{r}^{2}}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A family of planes: $x-2y+z={r}^{2}$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+z+3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ -{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+z+3 & =c \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+c-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we let $c-3=C,$ we get a family of elliptic paraboloids:\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C,\\,C\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-129196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A family of elliptic cones: ${z}^{2}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$"] ["text" "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$"] ["text" "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$"] ["text" "A family of hyperbolic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$"] ["text" "A family of planes: $z=x+3y+C$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following describes the $c$-level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}},$ where $c>0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1,\\,c\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-71582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A family of ellipsoids: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+{z}^{2}=1$"] ["text" "A family of hyperbolic paraboloid: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=z$"] ["text" "A family of hyperboloids of one sheet: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1$"] ["text" "A family of hyperboloids of one sheet: $cx-2cy+3cz-8=0$"] ["text" "A family of spheres: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A family of hyperboloids of one sheet: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & ={e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${e}^{c}={r}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surfaces form a family of spheres centered at the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-71578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,{e}^{-c})$"] ["text" "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,{e}^{c})$"] ["text" "A family of spheres centered at the origin"] ["text" "A family of surfaces given by ${z}^{2}+c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$"] ["text" "A family of surfaces given by ${z}^{2}+c={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A family of spheres centered at the origin"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c \\\\ z & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of curves:\n\n$$\nz={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c},\\,c\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-129203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A family of curves: $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+c)$"] ["text" "A family of curves: $z={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c}$"] ["text" "A family of curves: $z={e}^{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}+c$"] ["text" "A family of hyperbolic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+c$"] ["text" "A family of paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+c$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A family of curves: $z={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x-y+z-1}$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ={e}^{1-1+1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{x-y+z-1} & =1 \\\\ x-y+z-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,1)$ is the plane $x-y+z-1=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-129192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x-y+z+2=0"] ["math" "x-y+z+4=0"] ["math" "x-y+z-1=0"] ["math" "x-y+z-4=0"] ["math" "x-y+z=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-y+z-1=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point $P(3,1,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,1,6) & =\\frac{{6}^{2}}{{3}^{2}+9({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{18} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}) \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(3,1,6)$ is the elliptic cone\n ${z}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-129194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{z}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}=4{x}^{2}+36{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+2"] ["math" "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-129194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{z}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-36986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z)$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-36986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2(1)+3(0))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x+2y+3z & =3 \\\\ x+2y+3z-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,0)$ is the plane $x+2y+3z-3=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-36986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-36986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-36986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2y+3z+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3=0"] ["math" "x+2y+3z-3=0"] ["math" "x+2y+3z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}={3}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-36986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+2y+3z-3=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2}$ that passes through the point $P(4,2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,2,1) & ={4}^{2}+3(2-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=18.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(4,2,1)$ is the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-71596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point $P(2,1,22).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1,22) & =22-4(2)^{2}-6(1)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,1,22)$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c0f2dfa192cd8752f594b0ebe09a84bde76754e1c8b69b2faed0968240fe0366"} {"question" "q-71597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}"] ["math" "z=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-1"] ["math" "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}"] ["math" "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2"] ["math" "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "q-71597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/state.json" "file_sha256" "65d078bc3ab90cfc559503ec620331adf17c855b8c436f4eed9d73db9b1ba269"} {"question" "e-5967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+4y^2-z,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/example-5967.json" "file_sha256" "34407b19d9156e772939f645d85c74417fd14d95ed814ae0ebbcde233ef1fb0b"} {"question" "e-5967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z = x^2+\\dfrac{y^2}{{1/4}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/example-5967.json" "file_sha256" "34407b19d9156e772939f645d85c74417fd14d95ed814ae0ebbcde233ef1fb0b"} {"question" "e-5965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the family of surfaces that describe the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y, z) = \\ln(x^2 + 2y^2 - z^2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/example-5965.json" "file_sha256" "700df7d5415b5549479d5e418309cee76d0279da09f0427f20341ded45c9558a"} {"question" "e-5965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{e}^{c}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{e^c} + \\dfrac{y^2}{e^c/2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{e^c} =  1, \\qquad c \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/example-5965.json" "file_sha256" "700df7d5415b5549479d5e418309cee76d0279da09f0427f20341ded45c9558a"} {"question" "e-5966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z) = \\sin{xyz}$ that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/example-5966.json" "file_sha256" "5a670df6df45d62f273d90ee23b48afe3f35fef1b92cb3822c38f89ff5afca66"} {"question" "e-5966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n  f\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)&= \\sin \\left(\\pi\\cdot 1 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = 1}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡xyz & =1 \\\\ xyz & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ is the surface $xyz= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi k,$ where $k$ is any integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042953/example-5966.json" "file_sha256" "5a670df6df45d62f273d90ee23b48afe3f35fef1b92cb3822c38f89ff5afca66"}] "usage" {"e-5966" [] "e-5965" [] "q-36986" [] "e-5967" []} "basis" 1590 "retractions" [[17592186129543 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x, y, z) = \\ln (x + 2y + 3z)$ that passes through the point $P(1, 1, 0)$."] [17592186128916 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+4y^2-z$, find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0$."] [17592186128916 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 0$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= c \\\\\nx^{2} + 4y^{2} - z &= 0 \\\\\nz &= x^{2} + 4y^{2} \\\\\nz &= x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{1/4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid\n\n$$\nz = x^2+\\dfrac{y^2}{{1/4}}\n$$"] [17592186128914 :question/problem "Find the family of surfaces that describe the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y, z) = \\ln(x^2 + 2y^2 - z^2)$."] [17592186128914 :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= c \\\\\n\\ln (x^{2} + 2y^{2} - z^{2}) &= c \\\\\nx^{2} + 2y^{2} - z^{2} &= e^{c} \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{e^{c}} + \\frac{2y^{2}}{e^{c}} - \\frac{z^{2}}{e^{c}} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{e^c} + \\dfrac{y^2}{e^c/2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{e^c} = 1, \\qquad c \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$"] [17592186128915 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z) = \\sin{xyz}$ that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$."] [17592186128915 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)&= \\sin \\left(\\pi\\cdot 1 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 1$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= c \\\\\n\\sin xyz &= 1 \\\\\nxyz &= \\frac{π}{2} + 2πk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point\n\n$$\nP\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis the surface\n\n$$\nxyz= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi k\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127053 17592186127054 17592186127055 17592186127056 17592186127057] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127052 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127052 :answer-field/id] [17592186127052 :answer-field/key] [17592186127052 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
